        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          <b>Objective:</b><div><br></div><div><span style="font-size: 14px;">Neurovestibular adaptations that occur while in microgravity induce performance decrements in astronauts when re-exposed to a gravitational environment. This includes spatial disorientation, motion sickness, postural and locomotion impairment, ataxia, and manual control decrements These deficits are expected to occur upon initial exposure to another gravity environment, such as descending to the surface of the moon or Mars. Due to the sensorimotor impairment occurring during gravity transitions, these adaptations have the potential to be catastrophic, impacting mission critical tasks such as piloted vehicle landing with the acute risk of loss-of-crew from an accident or loss of mission through forced abort scenarios.
This study proposes to develop, validate, and assess a system for detecting when astronauts may become disoriented in real-time, such that it can be used to trigger active countermeasures for piloted planetary landings.
The investigative team will accomplish this objective using the following specific aims:
1.
Validate the computational model’s predictions of pilot spatial disorientation, specifically for lunar landing.
2.
Characterize manual control decrements without a countermeasure.
3.
Demonstrate the benefits of triggering an active countermeasure in real-time using the computational model to predict spatial disorientation (SD).</span></div><div><span style="font-size: 14px;"><b><br></b></span></div><div><span style="font-size: 14px;"><b>Approach:</b></span></div><div><span style="font-size: 14px;"><br></span></div><div><span style="font-size: 14px;">Astronauts landing on the moon will be faced with a gravity transition that will challenge their sensorimotor systems. Specifically, during 3+ days of transit, they will reinterpret sensory cues to appropriate for the microgravity environment. Then, during the critical landing phase, they will first be exposed to a lunar gravity. While orientation perception within lunar gravity has been studied well, much less is known about how astronauts perceive their vehicle’s orientation during lunar landing following sensorimotor adaptation to microgravity. In order to test their hypothesis, two experiments (Experiment A and Experiment B) will be performed by the PI team.
In Experiment A, the PI team will revalidate their computational tool for the unique lunar landing-like motions following an analog for the gravity transition. This experiment aims to quantify pilot SD during simulated lunar landings following the analog microgravity transition. During this experiment, as subjects are experiencing lunar landing like motions, at various points, the motion will freeze. At this instant, subjects will be shown veridical vehicle orientation information and mentally compare their perceived orientation at the freeze to the veridical information in order to gain a sense of their own disorientation. Subjects will report their SD severity, using a visual analog scale. Once their SD severity is reported that trial is terminated. This will capture the magnitude of SD experienced at the SD freeze.
In Experiment B, the validated computational tool, that detects pilot SD in real-time to trigger and active countermeasure, the team will assess the pilot aiding system’s efficacy versus a control group.
To assess the efficacy of the triggering system, the team will use an adaptive display which will alert the pilot and draw their attention towards a display of the vehicle’s attitude and motion to actively help them reorient. In the team’s initial implementation, the countermeasure will remain active for a fixed period to help reorient the pilot and then will deactivate. Two subject cohorts will be tested in a between-subject design. A control group will perform the lunar landing manual control piloting task but receive no countermeasure. Computational tools will be used in the control group to predict when the pilot is likely disoriented, however it will not activate any countermeasure. This will allow the PI team to identify whether the trials in which their performance was most degraded where indeed those the computational tool predicted SD.
In a second cohort of subjects, the PI team will assess the potential benefits of the computational tool triggering the active countermeasure described above. The subject will continue to fly the DRD simulation until landing, abort, or crash. There will be 12 subjects in Experiment B’s control group and another 12 subjects in the cohort of subjects with the countermeasure, for a total of N=24 subjects.
The PI team expects that the countermeasure approach will help to avoid increasing the pilots’ workload and the tool will be able to identify when the pilot if suffering from SD, including Type I SD. This will likely help reduce workload by removing the burden from the pilot of always having to assess whether they are becoming disoriented, as well as trying to prevent the pilot from having to “fly through’’ severe SD. When the active countermeasure is triggered, it will aid the pilot, likely improving manual control performance, increasing SA of vehicle orientation, and resulting in fewer failed landings, compared to the control group.</span></div><div><span style="font-size: 14px;"><b><br></b></span></div><div><span style="font-size: 14px;"><b>Results</b>:</span></div><div><span style="font-size: 14px;"><br></span></div><div><span style="font-size: 14px;">The investigators aim to develop an approach to actively trigger countermeasures, so it may be integrated with other potential countermeasures (e.g., a vibrotactile vest, HUDs), potentially including those being proposed by other investigator teams. The PI team expects pilot aiding countermeasure system to mitigate manual control performance decrements, as compared to the control group, which performs the lunar landing piloting task without a countermeasure. It is expected that the countermeasure system will reduce pilot mental workload and increase situational awareness.</span></div>
